  "id": "DrugBank_DB00997 - MONDO_16248",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the pathogenesis of Familial Ovarian Cancer through a series of interconnected pathways involving cancer-related indications. The evidence suggests that Doxorubicin may exert its effects on ovarian cancer cells through mechanisms that are not fully elucidated, but it is associated with the treatment of Ovarian Mucinous Adenocarcinoma, Kaposi's Sarcoma, and Malignant Germ Cell Tumor Of Ovary, all of which are linked to Familial Ovarian Cancer. These associations are supported by clinical indications and drug–disease links, though the exact mechanisms by which Doxorubicin influences the development or progression of Familial Ovarian Cancer remain unclear. The presence of multiple indirect pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, with Doxorubicin potentially acting through diverse biological processes that are not yet fully characterized. The integration of these pathways underscores the need for further mechanistic investigation to clarify the role of Doxorubicin in Familial Ovarian Cancer.",
